{"type":"standard","title":"Negation","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Negation</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q190558","titles":{"canonical":"Negation","normalized":"Negation","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Negation</span></span>"},"pageid":161019,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Venn10.svg/330px-Venn10.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":330,"height":330},"originalimage":{"source":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/73/Venn10.svg/330px-Venn10.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","width":280,"height":280},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1374547769","tid":"67a2783c-aeda-11f1-aa79-bd68ba3a58ab","timestamp":"2026-09-12T18:47:29Z","description":"Logical operation","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Negation"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Negation","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Negation"}},"extract":"In logic, negation, also called the logical not or logical complement, is an operation that takes a proposition  to another proposition \"not \", written , ,  or . It is interpreted intuitively as being true when  is false, and false when  is true. For example, if  is \"The dog runs\", then \"not \" is \"The dog does not run\". An operand of a negation is called a negand or negatum.","extract_html":"<p>In logic, <b>negation</b>, also called the <b>logical not</b> or <b>logical complement</b>, is an operation that takes a proposition <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b4dc73bf40314945ff376bd363916a738548d40a\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.745ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> to another proposition \"not <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b4dc73bf40314945ff376bd363916a738548d40a\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.745ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span>\", written <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/5eb0d6c8752f8c7256d69c62e77dfe4c466dbe58\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:3.296ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span>, <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/9daca81525792364a725ec311d9d762a8a6956a9\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:3.554ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span>, <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/56ad46bf6f18ec059e93caf8cc3d2db843366b76\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:2.506ex;height:2.509ex\" /></span> or <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/d5e1bed5bc42d4e46dd9e5c7d2fc327927b87169\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:2.025ex;height:3.009ex\" /></span>. It is interpreted intuitively as being true when <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b4dc73bf40314945ff376bd363916a738548d40a\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.745ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> is false, and false when <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b4dc73bf40314945ff376bd363916a738548d40a\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.745ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> is true. For example, if <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b4dc73bf40314945ff376bd363916a738548d40a\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.745ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span> is \"The dog runs\", then \"not <span class=\"mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline\"><img src=\"https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/b4dc73bf40314945ff376bd363916a738548d40a\" class=\"mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.338ex;width:1.745ex;height:2.176ex\" /></span>\" is \"The dog does not run\". An operand of a negation is called a <i><b>negand</b></i> or <i><b>negatum</b></i>.</p>"}